        "math_academy_id": "q-365308",
        "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{4}{(0.54)}^{x+3}-2e\\ge e.$",
        "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{(0.54)}^{x+3}-2e & \\ge e \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{(0.54)}^{x+3} & \\ge 3e \\\\ {(0.54)}^{x+3} & \\ge 4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061[(0.54)^{x+3}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4e) \\\\ (x+3)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.54) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4e)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.54)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4e)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.54)} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4e)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.54)}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
